li><a href="#common-cpr-mistakes-and-how-to-avoid-them" class="text-sm text-slate-700 hover:text-slate-900 hover:underline">Common CPR Mistakes and How to Avoid Them</a></li><li><a href="#when-to-stop-cpr" class="text-sm text-slate-700 hover:text-slate-900 hover:underline">When to Stop CPR</a></li><li><a href="#essential-cpr-supplies-and-training-equipment" class="text-sm text-slate-700 hover:text-slate-900 hover:underline">Essential CPR Supplies and Training Equipment</a></li><li><a href="#faqs" class="text-sm text-slate-700 hover:text-slate-900 hover:underline">FAQs</a></li><li><a href="#sources-and-methodology" class="text-sm text-slate-700 hover:text-slate-900 hover:underline">Sources and Methodology</a></li></ol></nav><div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none mt-8"><p><strong>CPR (cardiopulmonary resuscitation) for adults involves calling 911, placing the heel of your hand on the center of the chest, pushing hard and fast at a rate of 100-120 compressions per minute at a depth of at least 2 inches, and delivering rescue breaths at a ratio of 30 compressions to 2 breaths. Hands-only CPR (compressions without breaths) is recommended for untrained bystanders.</strong></p>
